1.  INTRODUCTION

    The MODTRAN layer cloud/rain models have been upgraded, making them both
easier to modify and more flexible.  The cloud models affected are all the
cumulus and stratus type clouds, both with and without rain i.e., cloud/rain
models 1 through 10 (the only change to the cirrus models is the introduction of
improved layering).  Upgrades to these models include:

   ADJUSTABLE CLOUD PARAMETERS:
     - thickness, 
     - altitude, 
     - vertical extinction, 
     - column amounts, 
     - humidity, and 
     - scattering phase functions;

   DECOUPLING OF THE CLOUDS FROM AEROSOLS;

   INTRODUCTION OF ICE PARTICLES;

   USER-DEFINED WATER DROPLET, ICE PARTICLE AND RAIN RATE PROFILES;

   USER-DEFINED CLOUD SPECTRAL PROPERTIES; AND

   OUTPUT OF CLOUD/RAIN PROFILES AND SPECTRAL DATA TO TAPE6 (NOPRT = -1).

    The decoupling of the cloud and aerosol models has a number of implications.
Clouds and aerosols can co-exist at a single altitude, or clouds can be modeled
with no aerosol profiles included.  When clouds and aerosols co-exist, the cloud
water droplets, cloud ice particles and aerosol particles may all have different
scattering phase functions.  The single scatter solar contribution of each
component is properly combined (for multiple scattering a single effective phase
function is defined based on a scattering optical depth weighted Henyey-
Greenstein asymmetry factor; this is the same approach that is used to combine
the aerosol and molecular scattering contributions).

    Cloud profiles are merged with the other atmospheric profiles (pressure,
temperature, molecular constituent and aerosol) by combining and adding layer
boundaries.  Any cloud layer boundary within half a meter of an atmospheric
boundary layer is translated to make the layer altitudes coincide; new
atmospheric layer boundaries are defined to accommodate the other cloud
layer boundaries.  If the total number of required layer boundaries increases
above parameter LAYDIM (defined in PARAM.LST), a fatal error message is output
indicating the required value of LAYDIM.  Cloud water droplet densities, ice
particle densities and rain rates are all modeled as varying linearly with
altitude.

    Implementation of the cloud/rain model upgrades required changes to a large
number of the MODTRAN3 routines.  In addition, six new subroutines have been
developed:

  crdriv.f:    Drives set up of the cloud/rain models.

  crprof.f:    Defines cloud water droplet and ice particle density profiles and               rain rate profiles.

  cruprf.f:    Reads in user-defined profiles for cloud water droplet and ice
               particle densities and for rain rates.

  crmerg.f:    Merges cloud layer boundaries into the overall atmospheric layer
               boundaries.

  crspec.f:    Defines water droplet and ice particle spectral extinction
               coefficients, absorption coefficients and asymmetry factors.

  cruspc.f:    Reads in user-defined water droplet and ice particle spectral
               extinction coefficients, absorption coefficients, and asymmetry
               factors.

Modifications to the users instructions are described in Section 2.  Changes to
MODTRAN3 routines are detailed in Section 3.



                      2.  USERS INSTRUCTIONS

    The use of some of the original MODTRAN input variables has been modified to
accommodate the cloud/rain model upgrades.  Setting the CARD 1 input NOPRT to -1
now produces output of cloud/rain profile and spectral data.  The MODTRAN
interpretation of the CARD 2 input IHAZE has been expanded.  Previously, the
CARD 2 variable ICLD was ignored unless a haze model was selected (IHAZE=1-10);
now if ICLD equals 1-10, a cloud is defined with no aerosols if IHAZE is
negative.  Furthermore, if a haze model is selected (IHAZE>0) and ICLD is 1-10,
the aerosol profiles are defined for a cloud-free atmosphere (ICLD is
temporarily set to 0); after the aerosol model has been set up, a separate cloud
model is defined.  As before, if IHAZE is set to 0, no aerosol and cloud
profiles are included in the MODTRAN calculations.  The CARD 3A2 input G has
also been modified.  Previously, if solar scattering calculations were performed
with CARD 3A1 input IPH set equal to 0, G was used as the Henyey-Greenstein
scattering phase function asymmetry factor in the single scatter solar
calculations for all aerosols and clouds.  Now the CARD 3A2 input G is only used
for aerosols, and it is used to describe the aerosol phase functions in both the
single and multiple scattering calculations.  (Previously, the multiple
scattering calculation ignored the input value of G and simply used the internal
model data.)

    If a cirrus cloud model is selected, i.e., ICLD equals 18, 19 or 20, CARD 2A
inputs remain unchanged; however, the format has been modified for consistency
with the liquid water cloud models:

If ICLD = 18, 19 or 20

  CARD 2A:     CTHIK, CALT, CEXT, ISEED 
               FORMAT(3F8.3,I4)

As always, an input of 0 for CTHIK, CALT or CEXT yields the default value.  Now,
assigning a negative value to any of these CARD 2A variables also yields the
default value.

    The new cloud/rain data are input through an expanded CARD 2A and new input
lines, CARDS 2E1 and 2E2:

If ICLD = 1 - 10

  CARD 2A:     CTHIK, CALT, CEXT, NCRALT, NCRSPC, CWAVLN, CCOLWD,
               CCOLIP, CHUMID, ASYMWD, ASYMIP 
               FORMAT(3F8.3,2I4,6F8.3)

If ICLD = 1 - 10 and NCRALT   3

  CARD 2E1:    (ZCLD(I,0), CLD(I,0), CLDICE(I,0), RR(I,0), I = 1, NCRALT)
               FORMAT(4F10.5)

If ICLD = 1 - 10 and NCRSPC   2

  CARD 2E2:    (WAVLEN(I), EXTC(6,I), ABSC(6,I), ASYM(6,I),
               EXTC(7,I), ABSC(7,I), ASYM(7,I), I = 1, NCRSPC)
               FORMAT(7F10.5)

The meaning of each of these inputs is described in detail in the following
sections.

2.1  CARD 2A:  CTHIK, CALT, CEXT, NCRALT, NCRSPC, CWAVLN, CCOLWD,
               CCOLIP, CHUMID, ASYMWD, ASYMIP  [If ICLD =  1, 2, ..., 10] 
               FORMAT(3F8.3,2I4,6F8.3)

    Default values can be assigned to any of the CARD 2A variables by setting
them equal to negative nine.  All CARD 2A variables are set to their default
value with the input line

  -9.000  -9.000  -9.000  -9  -9  -9.000  -9.000  -9.000  -9.000  -9.000  -9.000

Note, a blank line will not generate the default values.  In fact, setting all
CARD 2A inputs to zero would produce an isotropic scattering ground-level cloud. 
    The variable CTHIK is the cloud vertical thickness:

     CTHIK >  0.          Cloud vertical thickness [km]
     CTHIK >= 0.          Use default cloud thickness

The cloud vertical thickness is defined as the altitude difference between the
highest and lowest cloud profile boundary altitude for which either water
droplet or ice particle density is positive.  This definition can be a little
confusing.  Consider, for example, the MODTRAN default altostratus cloud.  The
default thickness for this cloud is 0.6 km, with a 2.4 km base and 3.0 km top.
That is, the lowest cloud altitude with a positive liquid water droplet density
is 2.4 km and the highest cloud profile altitude with a positive liquid water
droplet density is 3.0 km.  But, the water droplet density for the altostratus
cloud is actually positive between 2.0 and 3.5 km; 2.0 km is the cloud profile
altitude just below 2.4 km and 3.5 km is the cloud profile altitude just above
3.0 km (more typical would be to have sharper boundaries for the model clouds, with densities falling to zero within tens of meters).

    If the altostratus cloud thickness is doubled by setting CTHIK to 1.2 km
(the default value is 0.6 km) and a new cloud base is not entered (see CALT
below), the cloud base would be kept at 2.4 km, but its top would be increased
to 3.6 km.  In fact, the entire cloud would be stretched. New zero liquid water
droplet density boundary altitudes would be defined at 1.6 and 4.6 km.  The
final effect would be to exactly double the vertical cloud particle column
densities.  For clouds with rain (ICLD = 6-10), the rain profiles above cloud
bottom (i.e. within the cloud) are stretched/compressed proportional to the
change in cloud thickness.  If the stretching of a cloud is so great that the
zero water particle density boundary is predicted to extend below ground, this
boundary is brought up to the ground; in this case, the increase in column
densities is less than proportional.  The 10 MODTRAN cloud/rain models are
derived from 5 distinct clouds.  The default thicknesses for these clouds are
listed in Table 1.







    For cloud/rain models (ICLD = 1-10), the input CALT is the cloud base
altitude relative to ground level:

         CALT >= 0.      Cloud base altitude relative to ground level [km]
         CALT <  0.      Use default cloud base altitude

This differs from the meaning of CALT in the cirrus cloud models (ICLD = 18, 19
or 20) which define base altitude relative to sea level.  Note that a value of
zero translates the cloud down to the ground; the user must enter a negative
altitude to have the cloud automatically placed at the default altitude.  As
noted above, base altitude is defined as the lowest cloud profile altitude for
which either the water droplet or ice particle density is positive.

    If there is a non-constant rain profile below a cloud, that profile is
stretched or compressed depending upon whether the base altitude is increased or
decreased.  If the cloud base is lowered too much, the predicted zero water
particle altitude can drop below ground.  In this case, the zero water particle
altitude is defined to be the ground, and the rain rate is assumed to vary
linearly altitude from ground level to cloud base.  The default cloud base for
each of the 5 MODTRAN water droplet cloud models is listed in Table 1.

    The input CEXT is the cloud liquid water droplet and ice particle vertical
extinction:

         CEXT >  0.      Cloud water particle vertical extinction [km-1]
         CEXT <= 0.      Do not scale extinction coefficients

The vertical cloud optical depth is defined as the product of CEXT and CTHIK.
For cirrus clouds, CEXT is always referenced to the center of the visible
spectrum, 0.55 microns.  For the cloud/rain models, ICLD = 1-10, CEXT is defined
for wavelength CWAVLN (see below).  Thus, if you are studying long-wave cloud
spectra, you can now directly input the long-wave extinction.  Within the code,
CEXT is used to scale the extinction (and absorption) coefficient curves.  The
ratio of the input optical depth (CEXT * CTHIK) to the calculated optical depth
(the product of column density and extinction coefficient at CWAVLN, summed for
both liquid water droplets and ice particles) is determined.  The extinction and
absorption coefficients at all frequencies are multiplied by this ratio.  The
default cloud extinction at 0.55 µm for each of the 5 MODTRAN liquid water
droplet model clouds is listed in Table 1.

    The input NCRALT is the number of layer boundary altitudes if a user-defined
cloud/rain profile is being input:

         NCRALT   3     Number of layer boundary altitudes in user-defined
                        cloud/rain profile (READ CARDS 2E1)

         NCRALT < 3     Use default cloud profile for ICLD

The maximum allowed value for NCRALT is 16, parameter NZCLD in PARAM.LST; this
value can be increased, but this change requires some modification of block data
MDTA.  NCRALT must be at least 3 to define the cloud base, the cloud top, and
the highest boundary altitude for which the water droplet and ice particle
densities must be zero.   It is generally recommended that the altitude below
which cloud densities are zero also be included in the cloud profile.  If this
altitude is not entered, MODTRAN assumes that the cloud densities drop to zero
1 meter below the cloud base.

    If user-defined cloud spectral data is input, NCRSPC is the number of
wavelength entries:

     NCRSPC   2     Number of wavelength at which cloud spectral data is being
                    entered (READ CARDS 2E2)

     NCRSPC < 2     Use default spectral data for ICLD

NCRSPC must be at least 2 so the minimum and maximum wavelengths do not
coincide.  A maximum of 101 wavelengths (parameter MXWVLN in PARAM.LST) may
be input; this limit may be increased by simply increasing MXWVLN and
re-compiling the code.  As noted above, the CWAVLN input is the reference
wavelength used in defining cloud vertical extinction:

     CWAVLN   0.2 and         Reference wavelength for defining
     CWAVLN   200.0           cloud vertical extinction [ m]

     CWAVLN < 0.2 or          The reference wavelength for defining
     CWAVLN > 200.0           cloud vertical extinction is 0.55  m

The variable CWAVLN is only used if a positive value for CEXT is input.
Furthermore, if CWAVLN is outside the spectral range of user-defined cloud
spectral data (CARD 2E2), a fatal error message is logged and execution
terminated.

  The input CCOLWD is the water droplet (WD) cloud vertical column density:

     CCOLWD >= 0.    Cloud liquid water droplet vertical column density or
                     amount [KM GM / M3]

     CCOLWD <  0.    Do not scale the water droplet densities

MODTRAN determines the ratio of the input water droplet vertical column density
to the column density calculated from the input cloud base and thickness, and
from the default water droplet densities.  Then all the water droplet densities
are scaled by this ratio so that the desired column amount results.

    It should be noted that if the cloud being modeled only has liquid water and
a positive cloud vertical extinction, CEXT, is input, then the MODTRAN predicted
path transmittances and radiances are independent of CCOLWD.  An increase in
liquid water droplet column amount simply produces a proportional decrease in
spectral extinction and absorption coefficients.  However, if the spectral data
are not being scaled to give a particular vertical extinction, increasing column
density will decrease transmission through the cloud.  Furthermore, if the cloud
consists of both liquid water droplets and ice particles, CCOLWD can be used to
customize the relative contribution from the two particle types.  The default
cloud water droplet column densities for the 5 MODTRAN liquid water clouds are
listed in Table 1.

    The input CCOLIP is the ice particle (IP) cloud vertical column density:

     CCOLIP   0.    Cloud ice particle vertical column density or
                    amount [KM GM / M3]

     CCOLIP < 0.    Do not scale the ice particle densities

Generally, CCOLIP is used to scale ice particle density the same way CCOLWD is
used to scale water droplet density.  However, two points should be noted:
(1) The MODTRAN cumulus and stratus type clouds (ICLD = 1 - 10) do not contain
ice particles; thus, only user-defined cloud profiles (see CARD 2E1 below) can
be scaled using CCOLIP.  (2) If both CCOLWD and CCOLIP are zero, they are both
reset to -1; it does not make sense to define a cloud with no liquid water
droplets or ice particles.

    The input CHUMID is the relative humidity at all layer boundaries with
either a positive rain rate or a positive cloud density:

     CHUMID >  0. and          Cloud/rain relative humidity [%]
     CHUMID <= 105.

     CHUMID <= 0. or           Assume 100% relative humidity at cloud/rain
     CHUMID >  105.            layer boundaries

As much as 5% super saturation is permitted, and clouds with 0% relative
humidity are forbidden.

    The input ASYMWD is the Henyey-Greenstein phase function asymmety factor for
scattering by cloud liquid water droplets:

      ASYMWD  < 1.        Water droplet Henyey-Greenstein scattering phase
			  function asymmetry factor at all wavelengths

      ASYMWD    1.        Use user-defined or model spectral asymmetry factors
			  for scattering by cloud liquid water droplets

Even if the user inputs spectral asymmetry factors using CARDS 2E2, MODTRAN uses
the ASYMWD value if its absolute value is less than one.  In general, it is
recommended that the model asymmetry factors be used.

    The input ASYMIP is the Henyey-Greenstein phase function asymmety factor for
scattering by cloud ice particles:

      ASYMIP  < 1.            Ice particle Henyey-Greenstein scattering phase
			      function asymmetry factor at all wavelengths

      ASYMIP    1.            Use user-defined or model spectral asymmetry
			      factors for scattering by ice particles

In general, it is recommended that the default model (standard cirrus) asymmetry
factors be used.

2.2  CARD 2E1: (ZCLD(I,0), CLD(I,0), CLDICE(I,0), RR(I,0), I = 1, NCRALT) 
               [ICLD = 1 - 10, NCRALT > 2]

    The CARD 2E1 inputs are used to set up user-defined cloud/rain profiles.  If
the CARD 2A inputs CTHIK, CALT, CCOLWD and CCOLIP are all assigned negative
values, MODTRAN calculations are performed using the user-defined cloud/rain
profiles exactly as input.  However, the CARD 2A variables can be used to study
the effect of changing the input cloud's thickness, altitude or column amounts.
One could perform the identical sensitivity analysis by explicitly altering the
user-defined profiles, but it is clearly convenient to be able to simply modify
the CARD 2A inputs.

    The cloud/rain layer boundary altitudes are entered into array ZCLD:

     ZCLD(I,0)      Altitude above ground level of layer boundary I for the
                    user-defined cloud/rain profile [KM]

ZCLD(1,0) can be 0., and this is necessary if it is raining on the ground.  The
model also allows the cloud to actually sit on the ground.  The ZCLD must
monotonically increase.  Also, a fatal error will result if the highest cloud
altitude, ZCLD(NCRALT,0), is above the top of the MODTRAN atmosphere (100 km
above sea-level for the model atmospheres).

    Liquid water droplet densities are entered into array CLD:

     CLD(I,0)       Liquid water droplet density at altitude ZCLD(I,0) [GM/M3]

The liquid water droplet densities cannot be negative.  MODTRAN models the
densities as varying linearly between altitudes.  The entire CLD array is scaled
if the CARD 2A variable CCOLWD is assigned a non-negative value.

    Ice particle densities are entered into array CLDICE:

     CLDICE(I,0)          Ice particle density at altitude ZCLD(I,0) [GM / M3]

The ice particle densities cannot be negative.  MODTRAN models the densities as
varying linearly between altitudes.  The entire CLDICE array is scaled if the
CARD 2A variable CCOLIP is assigned a non-negative value.

    Rain rates are entered into array RR:

     RR(I,0)        Rain rate at altitude ZCLD(I,0) [MM/HR]

The rain rates can not be negative.  If a rain rate is entered through CARD 2
(variable RAINRT), that constant rain rate supersedes the user-defined rain rate
profile.  Thus, if a user-defined rain rate profile is desired, variable RAINRT
must not be positive.

2.3  CARD 2E2: (WAVLEN(I), EXTC(6,I), ABSC(6,I), ASYM(6,I),
            EXTC(7,I), ABSC(7,I), ASYM(7,I), I = 1, NCRSPC)
            [ICLD = 1 - 10, NCRSPC > 1]

    The CARD 2E2 variables are used to input user-defined cloud spectral data.
If the CARD 2A inputs CEXT, ASYMWD and ASYMIP are all outside their accepted
range, MODTRAN uses the spectral data exactly as input.  However, if a positive
vertical cloud extinction, CEXT, is input, the extinction and absorption
coefficients curves are scaled.  Similarly, if the CARD 2A asymmetry factors
ASYMWD and ASYMIP have magnitude less than one, they supersede the ASYM(6,I) and
ASYM(7,I) values, respectively.

    The wavelength grid for the spectral data is input into array WAVLEN:

     WAVLEN(I)      Wavelength I in the spectral grid [microns]

The first wavelength, WAVLEN(1), can be as low as 0.0 microns, and the
wavelengths must be entered in increasing order.  Although MODTRAN transmittance
and radiance calculations are performed on a constant frequency [CM-1] grid,
wavelength [ m] interpolation is always used for cloud spectral data.  If a
positive vertical cloud extinction (CEXT) is input on CARD 2A, the reference
wavelength (CWAVLN or 0.55 µm) must be between WAVLEN(1) and WAVLEN(NCRSPC)
inclusive, or else MODTRAN execution will be terminated with a error message.

    The liquid water droplet extinction coefficients are input into EXTC(6,I):

     EXTC(6,I)      Liquid water droplet extinction coefficient at wavelength
                    WAVLEN(I)  [KM-1 M3 / GM]

If a negative value is input, EXTC(6,I) is assigned the wavelength interpolated
extinction coefficient from the default data for cloud model ICLD.

    The liquid water droplet absorption coefficients are input into ABSC(6,I):

     ABSC(6,I)      If positive:   liquid water droplet absorption coefficient
                                   at wavelength WAVLEN(I)  [KM-1 M3 / GM]

                    If negative:   liquid water droplet scattering albedo minus
                                   1 at wavelength WAVLEN(I)

If the input value for ABSC(6,I) is less than -1 or if it exceeds the extinction
coefficient at WAVLEN(I), ABSC(6,I) is calculated by first determining the
default absorption to extinction ratio for cloud model ICLD, and then
multiplying EXTC(6,I) by this ratio.  This is equivalent to assuming that the
liquid water model cloud single scatter albedo (omega) should be used to
determine the absorption coefficient.  A negative value for ABSC(6,I) not less
than -1 is taken to be the negative of one minus the liquid water droplet
scattering albedo, -(1-omega).

    The liquid water droplet Henyey-Greenstein scattering phase function
asymmetry factors are input into ASYM(6,I):

     ASYM(6,I)      Liquid water droplet Henyey-Greenstein scattering phase
                    function asymmetry factor at wavelength WAVLEN(I)

These inputs are ignored if the magnitude of the CARD 2A input ASYMWD is less
than one.  If ASYM(6,I) is also not between -1. and 1., ASYM(6,I) is assigned
the wavelength interpolated value from cloud model ICLD.

    The ice particle extinction coefficients are input into EXTC(7,I):

     EXTC(7,I)      Ice particle extinction coefficient at wavelength
                    WAVLEN(I)  [KM-1 M3 / GM]

If a negative value is input, EXTC(7,I) is assigned the wavelength interpolated
extinction coefficient from the standard cirrus cloud model (ICLD=18).

    The ice particle absorption coefficients are input into ABSC(7,I):

     ABSC(7,I)      If positive:   ice particle absorption coefficient at
                    wavelength WAVLEN(I)  [KM-1 M3 / GM]

                    If negative:   ice particle scattering albedo minus 1 at
                    wavelength WAVLEN(I)

If the input value for ABSC(7,I) is less than -1 or if it exceeds the extinction
coefficient at WAVLEN(I), ABSC(7,I) is calculated by first determining the
default absorption to extinction ratio for the standard cirrus cloud model
(ICLD=18), and then multiplying EXTC(7,I) by this ratio.  This is equivalent to
assuming that the standard cirrus cloud model single scatter albedo (omega)
should be used to determine the absorption coefficient.  A negative value for
ABSC(7,I) not less than -1 is taken to be the negative of one minus the ice
particle scattering albedo, -(1-omega).

  The ice particle Henyey-Greenstein asymmetry factors are input into ASYM(7,I):

     ASYM(7,I)      Ice particle Henyey-Greenstein scattering phase function
                    asymmetry factor at wavelength WAVLEN(I)

These inputs are ignored if the magnitude of the CARD 2A input ASYMIP is less
than one.  If ASYM(7,I) is also not between -1. and 1., ASYM(7,I) is assigned
the wavelength interpolated value from the standard cirrus cloud model (ICLD=18)



                           3.  CODE MODIFICATIONS

    Five new parameters were introduced into the PARAM.LST file to
facilitate integration of the cloud/rain model upgrades (default values are in
brackets):

     NZCLD,     the number of built-in cloud/rain layer boundaries [16],
     KMAX,      the number of MODTRAN species (extinction sources) [67],
     NAER,      the number of aerosol/cloud profiles [7],
     NWAVLN,    the number of wavelengths in built-in spectral data [47], and
     MXWVLN,    the maximum number of user-defined wavelength points for
                cloud/rain spectral data [101].

NZCLD is also the maximum number of profile layer boundaries allowed for
user-defined cloud profiles.  KMAX was removed from common /CNTRL/ and increased
to 67; liquid water droplets are species 66 and ice particles are species 67.
The seven aerosol/cloud profiles are the 

  1. boundary layer aerosol, 

  2. tropospheric aerosol,

  3. stratospheric aerosol,

  4. mesospheric aerosol, 

  5. cirrus clouds, 

  6. liquid water droplet clouds, and

  7. ice particle clouds.

The NWAVLN wavelengths are defined in block data EXTDTA.  CARD 2A input NCRSPC
cannot exceed parameter MXWVLN, but MXVWLN can be increased simply by increasing
it value in the PARAM.LST file and re-compiling the CODE.

    Many changes were made to MODTRAN common blocks.  The modified and new
common blocks are listed in Table 2.  Introduction of the new parameters
affected many of the original MODTRAN common blocks.  Generally, the same
variable names were used in each occurrence of the modified common blocks.
These changes are:

The MODTRAN unlabeled common was named common block /BASE/.

The new CARD 2A inputs were added to the /CARD2A/.

A /CARD3A/ common block was introduced to pass the aerosol asymmetry factor G to
routine AEREXT.

Dimensioning corrections were made to /DEAMT/.

Common block /CLDRN/ was introduced to pass cloud/rain profile data among the
new cloud model routines.

The /LAY5/ arrays were enlarged to include water droplet and ice particle
optical depths.

And, finally, phase function vectors PR(LAYTWO) and PA(LAYTWO) in /SOLS/ were
replaced by array PHASFN(LAYTWO,4); the four scattering sources are (1) Rayleigh
(molecular), (2) aerosols, (3) liquid water droplets, and (4) ice particles.

    Over 60 MODTRAN routines were modified.  Almost half of these routines were
modified only because of changes in the common blocks, introduction of the new
parameters, and/or the updates to comment cards.  The routines with these minor
changes are:

  ABCDTA  ATMCON      BMDATA    BMFLUX    BMLDX     BMLOAD
  BMTRAN  CIRR18      CIRRUS    CONVRT    DEFALT    DESATT
  DSTDTA  EXABIN      EXTDTA    FLXADD    IRFXN     MAPMS
  MARINE  MSRAD       RAIN      RDEXA     RDNSM     SHADE
  RHOEPS  TANHT       VSANSM    XINTRP    XMLATM    XPROF

Additional minor changes to other routines are listed with a terse description
of the modifications in Table 3.  Subroutine EQULWC was completely dropped since
all it did was define an array that was never used.

    Nine routines were completely re-written.  New versions of (1) AEREXT,
(2) AERTMP, (3) LAYCLD, and (4) MDTA were required for the integration of the
cloud model upgrades.  Routines (5) EXPINT and (6) HENGNS were changed from
subroutines to real functions.  The remaining three routines, (7) BMERFU,
(8) DBLTX, and (9) SMGEO, were all replaced with more accurate versions.

    These modified MODTRAN routines are in only five of the MODTRAN bundled
files: PARAM.LST, driver.f modtran3.f, bmtran.f and stdmdl.f.  In addition,
the 6 new cloud/rain routines have been bundled into a file designated clouds.f;
this file must be added to the MODTRAN makefile.  With these new files, the user
can run the upgraded MODTRAN cloud models.
